= Argument — Simple =

In logic, an '''argument''' is a group of statements where some statements give reasons for another statement.

The supporting statements are called '''premises'''.

The statement being supported is the '''conclusion'''.

An argument is not the same thing as people fighting or disagreeing.

Some arguments are deductive, where the conclusion must follow.

Others are inductive or abductive, where the premises support the conclusion without proving it completely.

A convincing argument is not automatically a good argument. Logic asks whether the reasons actually support the conclusion.

